'''Devin Armani Booker''' (born October 30, 1996) is an American professional [[basketball]] player for the [[Phoenix Suns]] of the [[National Basketball Association]] (NBA). He played one season of [[college basketball]] for the [[Kentucky Wildcats men's basketball|Kentucky Wildcats]], winning [[Southeastern Conference|SEC]] Sixth Man of the Year before being drafted 13th overall by the Suns in the first round of the [[2015 NBA draft]]. Booker is a five-time [[NBA All-Star|All-Star]], a two-time [[All-NBA Team|All-NBA]] member, and is the Suns' [[List of NBA franchise career scoring leaders|all-time leading scorer]]. He has also won gold medals with the [[2020 United States men's Olympic basketball team|2020]] and [[2024 United States men's Olympic basketball team|2024 U.S. Olympic team]]s.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.olympics.com/en/paris-2024/athlete/devin-booker_1954687 |title=BOOKER Devin |work=[[Paris 2024 Olympics]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241006162535/https://olympics.com/en/paris-2024/athlete/devin-booker_1954687 |archive-date=October 6, 2024}}</ref>

The son of former basketball player [[Melvin Booker]], Devin debuted with Phoenix at 18 years old and quickly became one of the league’s top scorers. At 20, he became the [[NBA regular season records#Rookie and age-related records|youngest player]] to score over 60 points in a game, finishing with a Suns franchise record 70 against the [[Boston Celtics]]. At 22 years old, he became the youngest player in NBA history with consecutive 50-point games. Booker helped lead the Suns to the [[NBA Finals]] in [[2021 NBA Finals|2021]] and earned All-NBA First Team honors in 2022 after a franchise-best 64-win season. In 2025, at 28, he became the Suns’ all-time leading scorer.
